A||||3||Standard||For all you who have removed your A/C||||5||
Z||000000||Standard||07-24-2000||12:26 AM||||How do you know when there's no more pressure in the system?  I want to get rid of mine because it blows warm air, and I never use it anymore anyways.. I know there can't be that much pressure left in the lines, but I don't want to be removing a fitting and have it blow off in my face.<P>I also don't feel like paying to have it discharged when I'm pretty sure it's already gone.. but if it happened to develop a controlled leak somehow I don't think anyone would notice..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  <P>Also, what's the easiest way to pull the compressor out?  Any other things I can easily remove?  <P>Thanks!<P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 147,000 miles and counting!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Carrolles||07-24-2000||07:41 AM||||Mike,<P>Under the hood (where the spare tire lives) you will see a silver canister up against the firewall on the passenger side.  It has a black cap covering a shrader valve (like a tire valve stem).  Remove the cap and depress the pin in the center of the valve.  If there is pressure you will hear/see a bit of freon escape.<P>When I installed my new engine I installed a new a/c compressor also.  It has to be removed from out the bottom.  Put the car up on jack stands and work from underneath.  <P>Get it as high as you can to give your arms maneuvering room.  There is a heat shield attached to the cradle on the bottom near the right front corner that is held on by two or three hex head screws (10mm I believe).  Removing it gives better access to the lower bolts on the a/c belt adjusting bracket.  The upper right hand side bolt is accessed easier if you remove the r/r wheel and the inner fender liner.  You can then sit in the wheel well and see the front of the a/c compressor while looking towards the driver's side of the car.<P>The hose assembly and pressure switch connections can be disconnected from the rear of the compressor while laying under the car.<P>The belt only serves the a/c compressor so you won't have to worry about getting a shorter one to replace it.  I don't remember though whether the a/c belt or the alternator belt is on the outside.  You may have to remove both or just cut the a/c belt off.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||07-24-2000||01:54 PM||||Carrolles is right.  The heat shield on the corner of the cradle is held on by (2) bolts (10mm socket).  And the A/C belt is the inner one - you have to remove the alternator belt first  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> .  Oh, well.  <P>As long as you're dismantling the A/C, you might as well disconnect the refrigerant lines at the block on LHS of the engine compartment (one bolt), and pull the lines out, rather than try to disconnect hoses at the compressor.  That way, the hoses won't be hanging down loose.   <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 07-24-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000003||Standard||07-24-2000||04:26 PM||||Already took the AC belt off when I was replacing the alternator belt, so no problem there.<P>Thanks for the help guys!!  This should make it a little easier to get at that front manifold.. and I'll have space for the supercharger.. hehe.  Just wish I had the money..||||reg||1
